3 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |           