 \" encoded as NaN natively. For supervised learning, you might want\"\n156 |                 \" to consider sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingClassifier and\"\n157 |                 \" Regressor which accept missing values encoded as NaNs natively.\"\n158 |                 \" Alternatively, it is possible to preprocess the data, for\"\n159 |                 \" instance by using an imputer transformer in a pipeline or drop\"\n160 |                 \" samples with missing values. See\"\n161 |                 \" https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/impute.html\"\n162 |                 \" You can find a list of all estimators that handle NaN values\"\n163 |                 \" at the following page:\"\n164 |                 \" https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/impute.html\"\n165 |                 \"#estimators-that-handle-nan-values\"\n166 |             )\n167 |         raise ValueError(msg_err)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:assert_all_finite`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n170 | def assert_all_finite(\n171 |     X,\n172 |     *,\n173 |     allow_nan=False,\n174 |     estimator_name=None,\n175 |     input_name=\"\",\n176 | ):\n177 |     \"\"\"Throw a ValueError if X contains NaN or infinity.\n178 | \n179 |     Parameters\n180 |     ----------\n181 |     X : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n182 |         The input data.\n183 | \n184 |     allow_nan : bool, default=False\n185 |         If True, do not throw error when `X` contains NaN.\n186 | \n187 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n188 |         The estimator name, used to construct the error message.\n189 | \n190 |     input_name : str, default=\"\"\n191 |         The data name used to construct the error message. In particular\n192 |         if `input_name` is \"X\" and the data has NaN values and\n193 |         allow_nan is False, the error message will link to the imputer\n194 |         documentation.\n195 | \n196 |     Examples\n197 |     --------\n198