====\n 5 | \n 6 | This example showcases a simple scatter plot.\n 7 | \"\"\"\n 8 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 9 | import numpy as np\n10 | \n11 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n12 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n13 | \n14 | \n15 | N = 50\n16 | x = np.random.rand(N)\n17 | y = np.random.rand(N)\n18 | colors = np.random.rand(N)\n19 | area = (30 * np.random.rand(N))**2  # 0 to 15 point radii\n20 | \n21 | plt.scatter(x, y, s=area, c=colors, alpha=0.5)\n22 | plt.show()\n23 | \n24 | # %%\n25 | #\n26 | # .. admonition:: References\n27 | #\n28 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n29 | #    in this example:\n30 | #\n31 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n32 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/scatter_with_legend.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==========================\n  3 | Scatter plot with a legend\n  4 | ==========================\n  5 | \n  6 | To create a scatter plot with a legend one may use a loop and create one\n  7 | `~.Axes.scatter` plot per item to appear in the legend and set the ``label``\n  8 | accordingly.\n  9 | \n 10 | The following also demonstrates how transparency of the markers\n 11 | can be adjusted by giving ``alpha`` a value between 0 and 1.\n 12 | \"\"\"\n 13 | \n 14 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 15 | import numpy as np\n 16 | \n 17 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n 18 | \n 19 | \n 20 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 21 | for color in ['tab:blue', 'tab:orange', 'tab:green']:\n 22 |     n = 750\n 23 |     x, y = np.random.rand(2, n)\n 24 |     scale = 200.0 * np.random.rand(n)\n 25 |     ax.scatter(x, y, c=color, s=scale, label=color,\n 26 |                alpha=0.3, edgecolors='none')\n 27 | \n 28 | ax.legend()\n 29 | ax.grid(True)\n 30 | \n 31 | plt.show()\n 32 | \n 33 | \n 34 | # %%\n 35 | # .. _automatedlegendcreation:\n 36 | #\n 37 | # Automated legend creation\n 38 | # -------------------------\n 39 | #\n 40 | # Another option for creating a legend for a scatter is to use the\n 41 | # `.PathCollection.legend_elements` method.  It will automatically try to\n 42 | # determine a useful number of legend entries to be shown and return a tuple of\n 43 | # handles and labels. Those can be passed to the call to `~.axes.Axes.legend`.\n 44 | \n 45 | \n 46 | N = 45\n 47 | x, y = np.random.rand(2, N)\n 48 | c = np.random.randint(1, 5, size=N)\n 49 | s = np.random.randint(10, 220, size=N)\n 50 | \n 51 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 52 | \n 53 | scatter = ax.scatter(x, y, c=c, s=s)\n 54 | \n 55 | # produce a legend with the unique colors from the scatter\n 56 | legend1 = ax.legend(*scatter.legend_elements(),\n 57 |                     loc=\"lower left\", title=\"Classes\")\n 58 | ax.add_artist(legend1)\n 59 | \n 60 | # produce a legend with a cross-section of sizes from the scatter\n 61 | handles, labels = scatter.legend_elements(prop=\"sizes\", alpha=0.6)\n 62 | legend2 = ax.legend(handles, labels, loc=\"upper right\", title=\"Sizes\")\n 63 | \n 64 | plt.show()\n 65 | \n 66 | \n 67 | # %%\n 68 | # Further arguments to the `.PathCollection.legend_elements` method\n 69 | # can be used to steer how many legend entries are to be created and how they\n 70 | # should be labeled. The following shows how to use some of them.\n 71 | \n 72 | volume = np.random.rayleigh(27, size=40)\n 73 | amount = np.random.poisson(10, size=40)\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ==========================\n 3 | Scatter plot on polar axis\n 4 | ==========================\n 5 | \n 6 | Size increases radially in this example and color increases with angle\n 7 | (just to verify the symbols are being scattered correctly).\n 8 | \"\"\"\n 9 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n10 | import numpy as np\n11 | \n12 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n13 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n14 | \n15 | # Compute areas and colors\n16 | N = 150\n17 | r = 2 * np.random.rand(N)\n18 | theta = 2 * np.pi * np.random.rand(N)\n19 | area = 200 * r**2\n20 | colors = theta\n21 | \n22 | fig = plt.figure()\n23 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n24 | c = ax.scatter(theta, r, c=colors, s=area, cmap='hsv', alpha=0.75)\n25 | \n26 | # %%\n27 | # Scatter plot on polar axis, with offset origin\n28 | # ----------------------------------------------\n29 | #\n30 | # The main difference with the previous plot is the configuration of the origin\n31 | # radius, producing an annulus. Additionally, the theta zero location is set to\n32 | # rotate the plot.\n33 | \n34 | fig = plt.figure()\n35 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n36 | c = ax.scatter(theta, r, c=colors, s=area, cmap='hsv', alpha=0.75)\n37 | \n38 | ax.set_rorigin(-2.5)\n39 | ax.set_theta_zero_location('W', offset=10)\n40 | \n41 | # %%\n42 | # Scatter plot on polar axis confined to a sector\n43 | # -----------------------------------------------\n44 | #\n45 | # The main difference with the previous plots is the configuration of the\n46 | # theta start and end limits, producing a sector instead of a full circle.\n47 | \n48 | fig = plt.figure()\n49 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n50 | c = ax.scatter(theta, r, c=colors, s=area, cmap='hsv', alpha=0.75)\n51 | \n52 | ax.set_thetamin(45)\n53 | ax.set_thetamax(135)\n54 | \n55 | plt.show()\n56 | \n57 | # %%\n58 | #\n59 | # .. admonition:: References\n60 | #\n61 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n62 | #    in this example:\n63 | #\n64 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n65 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar`\n66 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_rorigin`\n67 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_theta_zero_location`\n68 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_thetamin`\n69 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_thetamax`\n70 | #\n71 | # .. tags::\n72 | #\n73 | #    plot-style: polar\n74 | #    plot-style: scatter\n75 | #    level: beginner\n76 | \n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 74 | ranking = np.random.normal(size=40)\n 75 | price = np.random.uniform(1, 10, size=40)\n 76 | \n 77 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 78 | \n 79 | # Because the price is much too small when being provided as size for ``s``,\n 80 | # we normalize it to some useful point sizes, s=0.3*(price*3)**2\n 81 | scatter = ax.scatter(volume, amount, c=ranking, s=0.3*(price*3)**2,\n 82 |                      vmin=-3, vmax=3, cmap=\"Spectral\")\n 83 | \n 84 | # Produce a legend for the ranking (colors). Even though there are 40 different\n 85 | # rankings, we only want to show 5 of them in the legend.\n 86 | legend1 = ax.legend(*scatter.legend_elements(num=5),\n 87 |                     loc=\"upper left\", title=\"Ranking\")\n 88 | ax.add_artist(legend1)\n 89 | \n 90 | # Produce a legend for the price (sizes). Because we want to show the prices\n 91 | # in dollars, we use the *func* argument to supply the inverse of the function\n 92 | # used to calculate the sizes from above. The *fmt* ensures to show the price\n 93 | # in dollars. Note how we target at 5 elements here, but obtain only 4 in the\n 94 | # created legend due to the automatic round prices that are chosen for us.\n 95 | kw = dict(prop=\"sizes\", num=5, color=scatter.cmap(0.7), fmt=\"$ {x:.2f}\",\n 96 |           func=lambda s: np.sqrt(s/.3)/3)\n 97 | legend2 = ax.legend(*scatter.legend_elements(**kw),\n 98 |                     loc=\"lower right\", title=\"Price\")\n 99 | \n100 | plt.show()\n101 | \n102 | # %%\n103 | #\n104 | # .. admonition:: References\n105 | #\n106 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n107 | #    in this example:\n108 | #\n109 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n110 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.legend` / `